              %li
                %a{:href => "#related_concepts"} Related concepts
              %li
                %a{:href => "#References"} References
          %h2#Idea Idea
          %p
            The description of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is traditionally mostly considered only in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+neighbourhood"} infinitesimal neighbourhood
            of the underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
            As such these descriptions are referred to as
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            (pQFT) since they describe only small (in fact: infinitesimal) “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbations"}> perturbations
            ” of a (hypothetical) free and classical field, instead of the fully interacting quantum fields.
          %p
            Since this very coarse (but remarkably successful) perturbative concept of quantum field theory has come to often be considered by default, one speaks of
            %em non-perturbative quantum field theory
            in order to amplify that the full theory is meant to be considered, not just the perturbative approximation.
          .float_right_image{:style => "margin: -40px 0px 20px 10px"}
            %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/NonPerturbativeParameterSpace-230613.jpg", :width => "360px"}/
              %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                (from
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#SS24IHH"} SS24, p. 3
          %p#BeyondInfinitesimalNeighbourhood
            %strong Beyond the infinitesimal neighbourhood of perturbation theory.
            More concretely, if we think of generic (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"}> Lagrangian
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theories"} quantum field theories
            as parameterized by
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"} Planck's constant
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mphantom
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi ℏ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phantom{-}\hbar
              %p
                expressing the “strength of
                = succeed "\u201D," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum effects
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constants"} coupling constants
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mphantom
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mi g
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phantom{-}g
              %p
                expressing the strength of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interactions"} interactions
                of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
          %p
            then
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
            is concerned (just) with computing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            as (just) a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+power+series"} formal power series
            in these
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            %em vanishing
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius+of+convergence"} radius of convergence
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "perturbative+quantum+field+theory#ReferencesNonConvergenceOfThePerturbationSeries"} there
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+neighbourhood"} infinitesimal neighbourhood
            of the origin in this parameter space (the infinitesimal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/halo"} halo
            of a single point) – all the rest of the parameter space is the region of non-perturbative quantum field theory.
          %p
            %strong As perturbation with non-perturbative effects.
            Passage from the perturbative to the non-perturbative description of QFT involves taking account of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effects"} non-perturbative effects
            invisible to perturbation theory.
          %p
            These arise notably from the presence of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/soliton"} solitonic
            field configurations (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton"}> instantonic
            , after
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+rotation"} Wick rotation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connected+topological+space"} connected
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum"} vacuum
            field configuration, since they are constituted by topologically non-trivial structures, e.g.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+potentials"} gauge potentials
            which are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connections
            on a topologically non-trivial
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+bundle"} principal bundle
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            (such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+monopoles"} Dirac monopoles
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+instantons"} Yang-Mills instantons
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+bundles+in+physics"} fiber bundles in physics
          %p
            Beware that this is relevant even on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ,
                        %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{1,d}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetimes"} spacetimes
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/contractible+topological+space"} contractible
            in themselves,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solitons"} solitons
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons"}> instantons
            ) are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+configurations"} field configurations
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            is constrained to “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vanish+at+infinity"}> vanish at infinity
            ”, which means that they are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cocycles"} cocycles
            in (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"}> differential
            )
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compactly+supported+cohomology"} compactly supported cohomology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/one-point+compactification"} one-point compactification
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∪
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn ∞
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)_{\cup \{\infty\}}
            of space. For instance
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons"} instantons
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4
            are effectively bundles
            %a{:href => "one-point+compactification#ExamplesSpheres"}
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mrow
                        %mo ∪
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mn ∞
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mn 4
                    %mo ≃
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^4_{\cup \{\infty\}} \simeq S^4
            (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "Yang-Mills+instanton#FromTheMathsToThePhysicsStory"} there
          %p
            In lack of a general prescription for non-perturbative
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+resummation"} Borel resummation
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resurgence+theory"} resurgence theory
          %p
            %strong Theory and reality.
            Beware the usual habit in theoretical physics jargon of conflating the map with the territory (the field
            %em theory
            with the actual fields “in nature” that the theory means to describe): Real quantum fields in nature are always non-perturbative (the experimentally observed values of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Planck%27s+constant"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℏ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coupling+constant"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            are not contained in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+neighbourhood"} infinitesimal neighbourhood
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/zero"} zero
            – this is a purely theoretical/computational concept) so that their description by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
            is, by design, just an approximation of their complete nature.
          %p
            %strong As non-analyticity.
            The distinction between nonperturbative and perturbative is much that between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+functions"} smooth functions
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Taylor+series"} Taylor series
            of their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derivatives"} derivatives
            at a given origin: The Taylor series is a “perturbative description” of the full smooth function, which in contrast is the full non-perturbative object. Indeed the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative QFT
            are imagined to be just such Taylor series of actual smooth functions (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
            = succeed "\u201D," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effects"} non-perturbative effects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℏ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hbar
            and/or
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi g
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} g
          %p
            Of course, special among all smooth functions are the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+functions"} analytic functions
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+neighbourhood"} open neighbourhood
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+perturbation+series"} Feynman perturbation series
            actually
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/convergence"} converges
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+number"} positive
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/radius+of+convergence"} radius of convergence
          %p
            %strong Axiomatization and construction.
            Among
            %em axiomatizations
            of non-perturbative field theory is
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+quantum+field+theory"} algebraic quantum field theory
                (AQFT) in terms of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+nets+of+observables"} local nets of observables
                with values in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star+algebra"}
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mo *
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
                  \-algebras
                (axiomatizing the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Heisenberg+picture"} Heisenberg picture
                of
                = succeed ")," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functorial+quantum+field+theory"} functorial quantum field theory
                in terms of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism+category"} cobordism category
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+representation"} representations
                (axiomatizing the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schr%C3%B6dinger+picture"} Schrödinger picture
                of
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
          %p
            Among
            %em construction
            methods of AQFT has been “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/constructive+field+theory"}> constructive field theory
            ”, which aims to make rigorous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analysis"} analytic
            sense of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/path+integrals"} path integrals
            in simple cases. This has worked for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field+theory"} scalar field theory
            such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phi%5E4+theory"}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^4
              \-theory
            in spacetime dimension 2 and 3, but (so far) not in dimension 4.
          %p
            There are also some partial tools available from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum mechanics
            such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+deformation+quantization"} strict deformation quantization
            (
            %a{:href => "C-star+algebraic+deformation+quantization#Rieffel89"}> Rieffel 1989
            ). Here it may be noteworthy that the established construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
            has been understood to be an example of the systematic process of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            called
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fedosov+deformation+quantization"} Fedosov deformation quantization
            (
            %a{:href => "perturbative+algebraic+quantum+field+theory#Collini16"}> Collini 16
            ). Since the non-perturbative version of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+deformation+quantization"} formal deformation quantization
            is
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+C%2A-algebraic+deformation+quantization"} strict C*-algebraic deformation quantization
            the latter might be a useful perspective on the problem of non-perturbative QFT (cf. e.g.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#SS23QOQF"} SS23
          %p#ExamplesInIdeaSection
            %strong Examples of nonperturbative QFT.
            Presently, non-perturbative quantum field theories have been constructed only for simple toy examples, notably:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theories"} free field theories
                (no interaction),
            %li
              %p
                in low
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
                (e.g.
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2d+CFTs"} 2d CFTs
                = succeed ")," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+net"} conformal net
            %li
              %p
                for
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+field+theories"} topological quantum field theories
                (see e.g. at
                = succeed ")," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+3d+Chern-Simons+theory"} quantization of 3d Chern-Simons theory
            %li
              %p
                for pure
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field+theory"} scalar field theory
                (most famously
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phi%5En-theory"}
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mn 4
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^4
                    \-theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "#Serone18"} Serone 2018
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (Examples of non-perturbative
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interacting+quantum+field+theory"} interacting
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field+theory"} scalar field theory
                  in
                  %em any
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
                  are claimed in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "AQFT#BuchholtzFredenhagen20"} Buchholtz &amp; Fredenhagen 2020
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+nets+of+observables"} local nets
                  of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+states"} vacuum states
          %p
            %strong Key application: Confinement/Mass-gap in QCD/Yang-Mills.
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} non-perturbative effect
            of paramount importance in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/experiment"} experimentally
            observed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            (and invisible to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+QFT"} perturbative
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap"} mass gap
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simple+Lie+group"} simple
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
          %p
            That this experimentally observed confinement mechanism is indeed somehow encoded in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+density"} Lagrangian density
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gluons"} gluons
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            is plausibly verfied currently (only) by computer simulation (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+QCD"}> lattice QCD
            ), but an analytic construction of a corresponding non-perturbative
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization+of+Yang-Mills+theory"} quantization of Yang-Mills theory
            has remained elusive enough to be pronounced one of the open “Millennium Problems” by the Clay Mathematics Institute (see
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "mass+gap#ReferencesMassGapProblem"} there
          %p
            However, qualitative understanding of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
            is given by an argument that separated
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
            become connected by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+nuclear+force"} strong nuclear force
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} field
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
            whose
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+tension"} string
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tension"} tension
            exerts an attractive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/force"} force
            on any pair of quarks, keeping them together and hence “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"}> confined
            ” inside a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadron"} hadron
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+state"} bound state
          %p
            The attempt to understand non-perturbative confined QCD and hence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+hadrodynamics"} quantum hadrodynamics
            as a theory of such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tube"} flux tube
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
            has been both the origin of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            as well as, after some detour through
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GUT"} GUT
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+principle"} holographic principle
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Polyakov+gauge-string+duality"} Polyakov gauge-string duality
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FQCD+correspondence"} AdS/QCD correspondence
            and at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "confinement#ViaSkyrmions"} confinement via AdS/QCD
          %p
            However, ironically also
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            has been mostly understood only in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbation+theory"} perturbation theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+QCD"} holographic QCD
            to require the assumption of a large (in fact: humongous) and hence unrealistic number of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark"} quark
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/color+charge"} colors
            (namely of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/color+branes"} color branes
          %p
            But for this string theory, at least, there are hints as to its non-perturbative completion, enough so that it has a working title (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"}> M-theory
            ”) albeit remaining elusive. See at
            %em
              %a{:href => "AdS-CFT+correspondence#SmallNCorrections"}
                AdS-CFT correspondence – small-
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                corrections
            for more on this.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strongly+correlated+system"} strongly correlated system
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} non-perturbative effect
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+resummation"}> Borel resummation
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/resurgence+theory"} resurgence theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+deformation+quantization"} strict deformation quantization
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+bootstrap"} conformal bootstrap
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton"} instanton
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superselection+sector"} superselection sector
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+string+theory"} non-perturbative string theory
          %h2#References References
          %blockquote
            %p
              (See also references at
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effect"} non-perturbative effect
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/soliton"} soliton
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instanton"} instanton
              = succeed ".)" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+instanton"} Yang-Mills instanton
          %p
            Motivation via the generic
            %a{:href => "perturbative+quantum+field+theory#ReferencesNonConvergenceOfThePerturbationSeries"} failure of convergence
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbative quantum field theory
          %ul
            %li#BakulevShirkov10
              Alexander P. Bakulev,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dmitry+Shirkov"} Dmitry Shirkov
              = succeed "," do
                %em Inevitability and Importance of Non-Perturbative Elements in Quantum Field Theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1102.2380"} arXiv:1102.2380
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://www.proceedings.com/22646.html"} ISBN 978-86-82441-30-4
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.mphys6.ipb.ac.rs/proceedings6.htm"} book webpage
          %p Monographs:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yitzhak+Frishman"}> Yitzhak Frishman
              ,
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacob+Sonnenschein"} Jacob Sonnenschein
              = succeed "," do
                %em Non-Perturbative Field Theory – From Two Dimensional Conformal Field Theory to QCD in Four Dimensions
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511770838"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511770838
              = succeed "&rbrack;." do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1004.4859"} arXiv:1004.4859
              %p
                open access (2023) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/9781009401654"}> doi:10.1017/9781009401654
                \&amp;rbrack;
              %blockquote
                %p
                  “Relativistic Quantum Field Theory has been very successful in describing strong, electro-magnetic and weak interactions, in the region of small couplings by using
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} perturbation theory
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                  approximation, techniques of conformal invariance, integrable model approach, supersymmetric models, string theory approach etc. In spite of this major effort the gap between the phenomenology and the basic theory has been only partially bridged, and the problem is still open. The goals of this book are to provide a detailed description of the tool box of nonperturbative techniques, to apply them on simplified systems, mainly of gauge dynamics in two dimensions, and to examine the lessons one can learn from those systems about four dimensional QCD and hadron physics.”
          %p
            and via
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+axioms"} Wightman axioms
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Franco+Strocchi"}> Franco Strocchi
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em An Introduction to Non-Perturbative Foundations of Quantum Field Theory
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199671571.001.0001"} doi:10.1093/acprof:oso/9780199671571.001.0001
              %blockquote
                %p
                  “The perturbative series is
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "perturbative+quantum+field+theory#ReferencesNonConvergenceOfThePerturbationSeries"} known to diverge
                  = succeed ")," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phi%5E4+theory"}
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi ϕ
                              %mn 4
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^4
                      \-theory
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi s
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s+1
                  dimensions (with
                  = succeed ")." do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi s
                          %mo ≥
                          %mn 3
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \geq 3
          %p
            with focus on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+physics+--+contents"} topological physics
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solitons"}> solitons
            ,
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Skyrmions"} Skyrmions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/instantons"} instantons
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monopoles"} monopoles
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+anomalies"} gauge anomalies
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roberto+Percacci"}> Roberto Percacci
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em Non-Perturbative Quantum Field Theory – An Introduction to Topological and Semiclassical Methods
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.22323/9788898587056"} doi:10.22323/9788898587056
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://library.oapen.org/bitstream/handle/20.500.12657/96025/9788898587056.pdf"} pdf
          %p Further collections:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerard+%E2%80%99t+Hooft"}> Gerard ’t Hooft
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Arthur+Jaffe"} Arthur Jaffe
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gerhard+Mack"} Gerhard Mack
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/P.+K.+Mitter"} P. K. Mitter
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Raymond+Stora"} Raymond Stora
                (eds.)
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Nonperturbative Quantum Field Theory
                %strong 185
                (1988) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4613-0729-7"}> doi:10.1007/978-1-4613-0729-7
                \&amp;rbrack;
            %li#Froehlich92
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J%C3%BCrg+Fr%C3%B6hlich"}> Jürg Fröhlich
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Non-perturbative quantum field theory – Mathematical Aspects and Applications
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1142/1245"} doi:10.1142/1245
            %li
              %p
                %em>
                  %a{:href => "https://events.mcs.cmu.edu/confinement-qcd-strings-workshop-2025"} Workshop on Non-perturbative Quantum Field Theory and Confinement
                , Carnegie Mellon University (May 28-31, 2025) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://events.mcs.cmu.edu/confinement-qcd-strings-workshop-2025/schedule/"}> talks
                \&amp;rbrack;
              %blockquote
                %p
                  (focus on
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
                  in
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
          %p
            Outlook from the point of view of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+perturbation+theory"} causal perturbation theory
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/locally+covariant+perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} locally covariant perturbative quantum field theory
          %ul
            %li#HollandsWald14
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stefan+Hollands"}> Stefan Hollands
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Wald"} Robert Wald
              = succeed "," do
                %em Quantum fields in curved spacetime
              %strong 574
              (2015) 1-35 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.2026"}> arXiv:1401.2026
              ,
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.physrep.2015.02.001"} doi:10.1016/j.physrep.2015.02.001
          %p
            The case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scalar+field+theory"} scalar field theory
            (such as
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phi%5En-theory"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ϕ
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^n
                \-theory
          %ul
            %li#Serone18
              %p
                Marco Serone, from 2:46 on in
                %em
                  A look at
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi ϕ
                          %mn 2
                          %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi^4_2
                  using perturbation theory
                (January 2018) &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=J4nxvY1rOhI"}> recording
                \&amp;
            %li#BuchholtzFredenhagen20
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Detlev+Buchholz"}> Detlev Buchholz
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klaus+Fredenhagen"} Klaus Fredenhagen
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    A
                    = succeed "-algebraic" do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi C
                              %mo *
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^\ast
                %strong 377
                (2020) 947–969 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.06062"}> arXiv:1902.06062
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/s00220-020-03700-9"} doi:10.1007/s00220-020-03700-9
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              M. Rovira, A. Parreño, R. J. Perry:
              %em A Variational Approach to Quantum Field Theory
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.17887"}> arXiv:2409.17887
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Relevance of non-perturbative methods in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/condensed+matter+theory"} condensed matter theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+materials"}> quantum materials
            ) and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+information+theory"} quantum information theory
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+quantum+computation"}> topological quantum computation
            ):
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Alvaro Ferraz,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kumar+S.+Gupta"} Kumar S. Gupta
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gordon+W.+Semenoff"} Gordon W. Semenoff
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Strongly Coupled Field Theories for Condensed Matter and Quantum Information Theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 239
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-35473-2"} doi:10.1007/978-3-030-35473-2
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/content/pdf/10.1007/978-3-030-35473-2.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Fulde"}> Peter Fulde
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Correlated Electrons in Quantum Matter
